      "math_academy_id": "e-21870",
      "problem": "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14183117/assets/37229c0b489a922d4249e69739928af6fd3aff8f886ab0c50d310f4171eb34b8.svg)\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14183117/assets/d195238fd57cc7586cc3d9785aa6ede6174111027d150b7ca54d4c8e653d1252.svg)",
